1
00:00:00,338 --> 00:00:02,963
This chip has over twenty thousand cores.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:05,654
And not one of them can think for itself.

3
00:00:05,836 --> 00:00:08,161
That's exactly why it's so fast.

4
00:00:08,737 --> 00:00:11,212
A CPU is built for decisions.

5
00:00:11,318 --> 00:00:15,558
A few brilliant cores, each spending
most of its silicon on prediction,

6
00:00:15,568 --> 00:00:20,243
caching, and control — to finish
one task as fast as possible.

7
00:00:20,619 --> 00:00:23,069
A GPU makes the opposite bet.

8
00:00:23,149 --> 00:00:26,464
It strips most of that cleverness
out, and spends the silicon

9
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:30,024
on raw arithmetic —
copied thousands of times.

10
00:00:30,518 --> 00:00:32,353
It doesn't even run your program.

11
00:00:32,363 --> 00:00:33,963
The CPU does.

12
00:00:34,103 --> 00:00:38,543
It copies your data into the GPU's
memory, then sends one small function,

13
00:00:38,553 --> 00:00:42,478
with a single order: run
this, a million times.

14
00:00:43,078 --> 00:00:47,353
Zoom into the chip, and you find one
block, stamped out again and again.

15
00:00:47,547 --> 00:00:50,332
NVIDIA calls it
a streaming multiprocessor.

16
00:00:50,342 --> 00:00:52,542
AMD, a compute unit.

17
00:00:52,728 --> 00:00:55,653
A flagship card has a hundred and seventy.

18
00:00:56,189 --> 00:00:58,689
Each copy of that function is a thread.

19
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:02,375
Threads are packed into blocks;
each block lands on one unit,

20
00:01:02,385 --> 00:01:05,935
which slices it into squads
of thirty-two, called warps.

21
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:09,666
Here's the trick: a warp
shares one instruction stream.

22
00:01:09,828 --> 00:01:13,992
Each instruction is fetched once…
then all thirty-two threads execute it

23
00:01:14,002 --> 00:01:16,428
together, each on its own data.

24
00:01:16,685 --> 00:01:19,810
Like a rowing crew: one
call, thirty-two oars.

25
00:01:20,436 --> 00:01:23,811
So those twenty thousand
cores aren't tiny CPUs.

26
00:01:23,875 --> 00:01:27,075
They're lanes of arithmetic,
marching in lockstep.

27
00:01:27,492 --> 00:01:31,417
Inside each unit, a scheduler
juggles dozens of warps.

28
00:01:31,577 --> 00:01:35,117
Every thread keeps its registers
right on the chip — so when one warp

29
00:01:35,127 --> 00:01:38,542
stalls on memory, another
takes over instantly.

30
00:01:38,552 --> 00:01:39,952
Zero cost.

31
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:42,725
And they stall on memory a lot.

32
00:01:42,965 --> 00:01:44,655
Registers are instant.

33
00:01:44,665 --> 00:01:48,405
A small shared scratchpad lets
threads in a block trade results.

34
00:01:48,415 --> 00:01:49,815
Then caches.

35
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:53,408
Then VRAM — feeding a flagship
chip over a thousand gigabytes

36
00:01:53,418 --> 00:01:54,743
every second.

37
00:01:54,945 --> 00:01:57,820
And often, that's still
the real speed limit.

38
00:01:58,467 --> 00:01:59,767
Now it clicks.

39
00:01:59,925 --> 00:02:04,540
A 4K frame is eight million pixels,
each running the same small program

40
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:05,840
on different data.

41
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:07,000
Perfect.

42
00:02:07,306 --> 00:02:11,071
A neural network is mostly
giant matrix multiplications:

43
00:02:11,081 --> 00:02:13,721
billions of identical multiply-adds.

44
00:02:13,731 --> 00:02:15,231
Also perfect.

45
00:02:15,809 --> 00:02:19,374
But make threads disagree —
half branch left, half right —

46
00:02:19,384 --> 00:02:22,334
and half the squad sits
idle while the others work.

47
00:02:22,556 --> 00:02:25,806
Make them wait on each other,
and the lanes go quiet.

48
00:02:26,466 --> 00:02:29,216
So a GPU isn't a faster CPU.

49
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:33,525
It's a machine for doing one thing
to an ocean of data, all at once.

50
00:02:33,868 --> 00:02:36,118
Give it that, and it's unstoppable.

51
00:02:36,286 --> 00:02:39,661
Give it anything else…
and your CPU still wins.
